    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "A map of city life",
     "texto": [
      "Today we talk about how a city is organised.\n\n- **The territory:** a big city is divided into **distritos** and **barrios**. There are also the town, the centre and the outskirts.\n- **The places:** the **biblioteca** lends books; the **museo** shows art or history; the **estación** is where trains and buses arrive; at the **oficina** paperwork is done.\n- **The people:** an **encargado** runs a place, an **empleado** works in it, a **conductor** drives the bus, a **viajero** carries his **maleta** and a **cartero** delivers letters.\n- **To describe:** a **céntrico** neighbourhood, a **provisional** document, a historic museum.\n- **Obligations:** they are said with **tener que**, **hay que** and **deber**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Tener que, hay que and deber",
     "texto": [
      "There are three ways to talk about obligations, all with the **infinitive**:\n\n- **tener que**: personal obligation. **Tengo que** rellenar un formulario.\n- **hay que**: general obligation, no person. **Hay que** llamar antes. The verb **hay** never changes.\n- **deber**: more formal. **Debe** rellenar este formulario.\n\nCareful with the negative: **no tienes que** ir means «it is not necessary»; **no debes** ir means «it is better not to go» or «it is not right»."
     ],
